#!/bin/sh
# Starts keeper's MCP server, or explains why it cannot.
#
# The plugin does not ship the binary. keeper holds database credentials and
# audits privileges, and an executable that arrives as a side effect of
# installing a plugin is not one anybody chose to trust — plugin distribution has
# no checksum story, and this one's whole subject is what is safe to hand over.
# So it is installed separately and verified against the release's SHA256SUMS.
#
# What this wrapper buys is the message below. Without it the failure is a bare
# ENOENT, which says nothing about what to do next.
set -eu

if command -v keeper >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    exec keeper mcp "$@"
fi

cat >&2 <<'EOF'
keeper is not on PATH, so this plugin has nothing to talk to.

Ask your agent to install it — the plugin ships a `keeper-install` skill that
checks what is already present, installs what is missing, and tells you the
parts it cannot do itself.

Or do it by hand:

  # from a release, verified
  https://github.com/mtch3n/keeper/releases
  sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS --ignore-missing
  tar xzf keeper_<version>_<os>_<arch>.tar.gz
  install -m755 keeper_<version>_<os>_<arch>/keeper ~/.local/bin/

  # or from source, with Go 1.27 and pnpm
  git clone https://github.com/mtch3n/keeper && cd keeper
  make build && install -m755 bin/keeper ~/.local/bin/

One binary, three entry points: `keeper` is the CLI, `keeper daemon` runs the
daemon, `keeper mcp` is this server. Check it with `keeper version`, which
prints both the client's version and the daemon's — they refuse each other on a
mismatch, so one number would be the wrong answer.
EOF
exit 1
